    "mechanism": {
      "summaryHtml": "",
      "detailsHtml": "<p><strong>Simple explanation:</strong> Amlodipine is a dihydropyridine calcium‑channel blocker that relaxes vascular smooth muscle to lower peripheral vascular resistance and blood pressure. By reducing afterload and preventing coronary artery spasm it decreases myocardial oxygen demand and relieves angina.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Molecular target:</strong> Inhibits transmembrane influx of Ca2+ through voltage‑dependent (L‑type) calcium channels in vascular smooth muscle and cardiac muscle; experimental data show binding at dihydropyridine and non‑dihydropyridine sites.</li>\n  <li><strong>Vascular selectivity:</strong> Greater effect on vascular smooth muscle than myocardium → prominent peripheral vasodilation with little direct negative inotropy at therapeutic doses.</li>\n  <li><strong>Onset kinetics:</strong> Largely ionized at physiologic pH with slow association/dissociation at the channel receptor, producing gradual onset and sustained effect.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Clinical consequences:</strong> Vasodilation lowers systemic vascular resistance (antihypertensive effect, sustained ≥24 hours with once‑daily dosing), reduces myocardial oxygen demand in exertional angina, and prevents coronary spasm in vasospastic angina.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Key pharmacokinetics (clinically useful):</strong> Peak concentrations 6–12 hours after oral dose; bioavailability ~64–90%; terminal half‑life ~30–50 hours; steady state in 7–8 days.</li>\n  <li>About 90% hepatic metabolism to inactive metabolites (primarily CYP3A); ~10% unchanged drug and ~60% metabolites excreted in urine.</li>\n  <li>Renal impairment does not substantially alter exposure; elderly patients, those with hepatic impairment, and some with moderate–severe heart failure have 40–60% higher AUC and may require lower initial dosing.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout clinical\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical note</span><p>Although small negative inotropic effects are seen in vitro, clinically important decreases in contractility are uncommon at usual doses.</p></div>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout warning\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical caution</span><p>Amlodipine is metabolized by CYP3A; strong CYP3A inhibitors can raise amlodipine levels. Amlodipine is a weak CYP3A inhibitor and can increase exposure to some substrates (eg, simvastatin, cyclosporine, tacrolimus); monitor levels and adjust doses as needed.</p></div>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout dose\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Dose guidance</span><p>Consider a lower initial dose in elderly patients, those with hepatic impairment, or patients with moderate–severe heart failure and titrate based on response and tolerability.</p></div>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout patient\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Patient counseling</span><p>Onset of peak blood levels is gradual (6–12 hours); take once daily. Full effect builds over days; steady state in ~1 week.</p></div>",
      "title": "Mechanism of Action",
      "visualPrompt": "Diagram of amlodipine blocking L-type calcium channels in vascular smooth muscle causing vasodilation",
      "aiEdited": true
    },
    "contraindications": {
      "summaryHtml": "<ul><li>Amlodipine besylate tablets are contraindicated in patients with known sensitivity to amlodipine.</li></ul>",
      "detailsHtml": "<h4>Contraindications</h4><p>4 CONTRAINDICATIONS Amlodipine besylate tablets are contraindicated in patients with known sensitivity to amlodipine. Known sensitivity to amlodipine ( 4 )</p>"
    },
