    "specialPopulation": {
      "summaryHtml": "",
      "detailsHtml": "<p><strong>Pregnancy</strong>: Human data are limited and do not establish risk for birth defects or miscarriage. Animal studies at very high doses showed reduced litter size, increased intrauterine deaths and prolonged gestation/labor in rats. Poorly controlled maternal hypertension carries clear risks (pre-eclampsia, growth restriction, preterm delivery); pregnant patients with hypertension should be closely monitored and managed.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Lactation</strong>: Amlodipine is present in human milk (median relative infant dose ~4.2%). No adverse effects in breastfed infants have been reported; effects on milk production are unknown. Consider the benefits of breastfeeding and maternal therapy when deciding to continue treatment.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Pediatric use</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Population</th><th>Usual dose</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>6–17 years</td><td>2.5–5 mg once daily</td><td>Shown effective for lowering BP in this age group</td></tr>\n    <tr><td>&lt;6 years</td><td>Not established</td><td>Effectiveness and dosing unknown</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n\n<p><strong>Geriatric use</strong>: Elderly patients have reduced clearance and a 40–60% higher AUC. Start at the lower end of the dosing range and titrate cautiously, accounting for comorbidities and concomitant medications.</p>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout dose\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Dose guidance</span><p>For older adults, consider lower initial dose and slower titration due to increased exposure; monitor blood pressure and signs of hypotension.</p></div>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout clinical\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical note</span><p>When treating pregnant patients, balance maternal blood pressure control against limited fetal safety data; monitor mother and fetus closely.</p></div>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout patient\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Patient counseling</span><p>If breastfeeding, inform patients that small amounts of amlodipine appear in milk and no infant harm has been reported, but discuss risks and benefits with the clinician.</p></div>",
      "title": "Special Population",
      "visualPrompt": "Rewrite Special Population section for amlodipine besylate (pregnancy, lactation, pediatric, geriatric). Keep to 120-220 words, include dosing table for pediatrics, callouts for dose/clinical/patient, no boilerplate, preserve facts from provided source.",
      "aiEdited": true
    },
    "clinicalEvidence": {
      "summaryHtml": "",
      "detailsHtml": "<p>Amlodipine has consistent randomized-trial evidence for lowering blood pressure and improving ischemic symptoms, with varied effects across populations.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Key findings</strong></p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Hypertension: 15 double-blind, placebo-controlled trials (~800 patients on amlodipine) showed 24‑hour BP lowering of about 12/6 mmHg standing and 13/7 mmHg supine, with a dose-related effect and no tolerance up to 1 year.</li>\n  <li>Pediatrics: 268 hypertensive children (6–17 y) had small systolic BP reductions (≈&lt;5 mmHg on 5 mg; ≈3.3 mmHg on 2.5 mg).</li>\n  <li>Chronic stable angina: 8 trials (n≈1,038) found increased exercise time (≈63 s with 10 mg; 38 s with 5 mg) and fewer angina attacks.</li>\n  <li>Vasospastic angina: one small RCT (n=50) reduced attack frequency (~4/week vs ~1/week for placebo).</li>\n  <li>Coronary artery disease: CAMELOT (n=1,318) showed fewer composite events driven by reduced hospitalizations for angina and revascularizations (HR 0.69); no clear reduction in MI or death. PREVENT showed no angiographic benefit.</li>\n  <li>Heart failure: short trials showed no clinical worsening; larger long-term trials found no mortality/morbidity benefit and more reports of pulmonary edema.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout warning\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical caution</span><p>Amlodipine does not reduce mortality in established systolic heart failure and has been associated with increased pulmonary edema reports; use cautiously in advanced heart failure and monitor closely.</p></div>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout clinical\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical note</span><p>Evidence supports amlodipine for BP control and symptomatic angina relief; benefits on hard cardiovascular outcomes are limited and largely reflect fewer angina hospitalizations and revascularizations rather than reduced MI or death. Many trials excluded patients with severe left main disease or low ejection fraction, and some study populations were predominantly male and white, which may limit generalizability.</p></div>",
      "title": "Clinical Evidence",
      "visualPrompt": "Summarize randomized clinical-trial evidence for amlodipine across hypertension, pediatric hypertension, chronic and vasospastic angina, coronary artery disease (CAMELOT/PREVENT), and heart failure; highlight benefits, safety signals (pulmonary edema), and key limitations.",
      "aiEdited": true
    },
    "practicalInformation": {
      "summaryHtml": "",
      "detailsHtml": "<p>Quick reference for pharmacy and nursing use.</p>\n<table>\n  <thead>\n    <tr><th>Formulation</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Notes</th></tr>\n  </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr><td>Oral tablets</td><td>2.5 mg, 5 mg, 10 mg</td><td>White to off‑white, round, beveled edge</td></tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Storage: store at 20°–25°C (68°–77°F); brief excursions permitted to 15°–30°C (59°–86°F). Dispense in a tight, light‑resistant container.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Overdose — first steps:</strong> initiate cardiac and respiratory monitoring and frequent blood pressure checks.</li>\n  <li>Supportive measures: elevate extremities, give judicious IV fluids, and consider vasopressors (for example, phenylephrine) if hypotension persists despite fluids.</li>\n  <li>Note: hemodialysis is unlikely to be effective due to high protein binding.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"medlens-callout warning\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical caution</span><p>Massive overdose can cause marked peripheral vasodilation, severe hypotension, and reflex tachycardia. Provide rapid hemodynamic support and monitor urine output.</p></div>\n<div class=\"medlens-callout patient\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Patient counseling</span><p>Store at controlled room temperature in the original container. Advise patients to seek immediate medical care for severe dizziness, fainting, or symptoms of very low blood pressure. Keep out of reach of children.</p></div>",
      "title": "Practical Information",
      "visualPrompt": "Photo of three white/off-white round beveled tablets labeled 2.5 mg, 5 mg, 10 mg with a prescription bottle; neutral background.",
      "aiEdited": true
    }
