    "contraindications": {
      "summaryHtml": "<ul><li>Acute liver failure or decompensated cirrhosis [see Warnings and Precautions ( 5.3 )] Hypersensitivity to atorvastatin or any excipients in atorvastatin calcium tablets.</li></ul>",
      "detailsHtml": "<h4>Contraindications</h4><p>4 CONTRAINDICATIONS Acute liver failure or decompensated cirrhosis [see Warnings and Precautions ( 5.3 )] Hypersensitivity to atorvastatin or any excipients in atorvastatin calcium tablets. Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, angioneurotic edema, erythema multiforme, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, and toxic epidermal necrolysis, have been reported [see Adverse Reactions ( 6.2 )]. Acute liver failure or decompensated cirrhosis ( 4 ). Hypersensitivity to atorvastatin or any excipient in atorvastatin calcium tablets ( 4 ).</p>"
    },
    "warnings": {
      "summaryHtml": "",
      "detailsHtml": ""
    },
    "sideEffects": {
      "summaryHtml": "",
      "detailsHtml": "<p>Common, generally tolerable adverse effects reported in trials include:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Nasopharyngitis (upper respiratory symptoms)</li>\n  <li>Arthralgia and other musculoskeletal pain (including myalgia, muscle spasms)</li>\n  <li>Diarrhea</li>\n  <li>Pain in extremity and urinary tract infection</li>\n  <li>Occasional dyspepsia, nausea, and insomnia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Less common but clinically important reactions are related to muscle and liver toxicity and, rarely, immune or severe hypersensitivity events. Higher-intensity therapy (eg, atorvastatin 80 mg) was associated with increased rates of persistent transaminase and creatine kinase (CK) elevations compared with lower doses.</p>\n<div class=\"medlens-callout warning\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical caution</span><p>Stop the drug and evaluate urgently if patients develop unexplained muscle pain or weakness, dark urine, signs of liver injury (jaundice, persistent nausea/abdominal pain), new or worsening generalized weakness or drooping eyelids, breathing difficulty, or a severe rash—these may indicate rhabdomyolysis, immune‑mediated myopathy, significant hepatic injury, or severe hypersensitivity.</p></div>\n<div class=\"medlens-callout clinical\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical note</span><p>Persistent transaminase elevations (>3× ULN on two occasions) occurred in ~0.7% overall in trials (≈0.2% at 10–20 mg, 0.6% at 40 mg, 2.3% at 80 mg). Small increases in fasting glucose/HbA1c and rare reversible cognitive complaints were reported.</p></div>",
      "title": "Adverse Reactions",
      "visualPrompt": "Concise infographic: common side effects (nasopharyngitis, arthralgia, diarrhea) vs serious signs to report (muscle pain/weakness, dark urine, jaundice, severe rash).",
      "aiEdited": true
    },
    "interactions": {
      "summaryHtml": "",
      "detailsHtml": "<p>Atorvastatin interactions chiefly increase its plasma levels (via CYP3A4 or transporter inhibition), raising myopathy/rhabdomyolysis risk. Below are practical actions grouped by clinical priority.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Avoid</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Cyclosporine, gemfibrozil, tipranavir + ritonavir, glecaprevir + pibrentasvir — avoid coadministration.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Use with caution</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Strong CYP3A4 inhibitors (many protease inhibitors, some HCV regimens, azole antifungals, macrolides), niacin ≥1 g/day, fibrates (other than gemfibrozil), colchicine — increased myopathy risk; consider alternatives and close monitoring.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n\n  <li><strong>Monitor/adjust</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Digoxin — atorvastatin can raise digoxin levels; monitor concentrations and clinical status when starting/stopping atorvastatin.</li>\n      <li>Oral contraceptives (norethindrone/ethinyl estradiol) — atorvastatin may increase exposure; consider when counseling.</li>\n      <li>Grapefruit juice — avoid large amounts.</li>\n      <li>Rifampin — can lower atorvastatin exposure; give drugs together to reduce variability.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout dose\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Dose guidance</span>\n<p>\nDo not exceed atorvastatin 20 mg/day with saquinavir + ritonavir, darunavir + ritonavir, fosamprenavir (±ritonavir), elbasvir + grazoprevir, letermovir, clarithromycin, or itraconazole. Do not exceed 40 mg/day with nelfinavir.\n</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout warning\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical caution</span>\n<p>\nThe primary hazard is increased statin exposure leading to myopathy/rhabdomyolysis. Monitor for muscle pain, weakness, or dark urine and check creatine kinase if symptoms occur—especially after starting or increasing doses of interacting drugs.\n</p>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout patient\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Patient counseling</span>\n<p>\nAdvise patients to avoid large amounts of grapefruit juice, report new muscle symptoms promptly, and tell clinicians about all prescription, over‑the‑counter, and herbal medicines (including antivirals and antibiotics). Inform patients on digoxin that monitoring may be needed.\n</p>\n</div>",
      "title": "Drug Interactions",
      "visualPrompt": "Compact summary table of atorvastatin drug interactions with categories Avoid, Use with caution, Monitor/adjust; emphasize myopathy risk and dose limits (20 mg/day, 40 mg/day) for specific antivirals and azoles.",
      "aiEdited": true
    },
