  "sideEffects": "<p>Adverse reactions to digoxin are dose-dependent and reported in roughly 5–20% of patients, with about 15–20% of events considered serious. Risk is lower when doses are within recommended ranges, serum concentrations are maintained in the therapeutic window, and interacting conditions or medications are managed.</p>\n\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Common / tolerability effects</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Gastrointestinal: nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain; rare reports of intestinal ischemia and hemorrhagic necrosis.</li>\n      <li>CNS: headache, weakness, dizziness, apathy, confusion; mood/mental disturbances such as anxiety, depression, delirium, and hallucination.</li>\n      <li>Other: gynecomastia with prolonged use; rare thrombocytopenia and maculopapular or other skin reactions.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n  <li><strong>Serious / urgent reactions</strong>\n    <ul>\n      <li>Cardiac toxicity (about one-half of serious events) including cardiac arrhythmias and digoxin toxicity. In a large trial, hospitalization for suspected digoxin toxicity occurred in ~2% of treated patients.</li>\n    </ul>\n  </li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout warning\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical caution</span><p>Suspected digoxin toxicity or new/worsening cardiac arrhythmias require prompt clinical evaluation. Toxicity is more likely at higher-than-therapeutic doses and when serum concentrations, drug interactions, or comorbid conditions are not addressed.</p></div>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout patient\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Patient counseling</span><p>Take digoxin exactly as prescribed, attend monitoring of serum levels as directed, and report persistent nausea, severe dizziness, confusion, fainting, or pronounced palpitations promptly.</p></div>",
  "warnings": "",
  "contraindications": "<h4>Contraindications</h4><p>4 CONTRAINDICATIONS Digoxin is contraindicated in patients with: Ventricular fibrillation [see Warnings and Precautions (5.1)] Known hypersensitivity to digoxin (reactions seen include unexplained rash, swelling of the mouth, lips or throat or a difficulty in breathing). A hypersensitivity reaction to other digitalis preparations usually constitutes a contraindication to digoxin. Ventricular fibrillation. ( 4 ) Known hypersensitivity to digoxin or other forms of digitalis. ( 4 )</p>",
  "formulation": "<h4>Dosage Forms &amp; Strengths</h4><p>3 DOSAGE FORMS &amp; STRENGTHS 125 mcg tablets are yellow, round, scored tablets imprinted with “JSP-544”. 250 mcg tablets are white, round, scored tablets imprinted with “JSP-545”. Scored Tablets 125 and 250 mcg</p><h4>How Supplied</h4><p>16 HOW SUPPLIED/STORAGE AND HANDLING Digoxin Tablets, USP, 250 mcg (0.250 mg), Scored I.D. Imprint JSP-545 (white): NDC 71335-3156-1: 30 Tablets in a BOTTLE NDC 71335-3156-2: 60 Tablets in a BOTTLE NDC 71335-3156-3: 90 Tablets in a BOTTLE NDC 71335-3156-4: 100 Tablets in a BOTTLE Store at controlled room temperature 15° - 25°C (59°F - 77°F) in a dry place and protect from light. Keep out of reach of children. Dispense in a tight, light-resistant container as defined in the USP. Repackaged/Relabeled by: Bryant Ranch Prepack Burbank, CA 91504</p>",
  "interactions": "<p>Digoxin has a narrow therapeutic index. Assess and monitor serum digoxin concentrations and for signs of toxicity whenever starting, stopping, or changing doses of interacting drugs.</p>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout warning\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical caution</span><p>Serious pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic interactions can occur. Consider alternative therapies or dose changes and increase monitoring when combining digoxin with interacting drugs.</p></div>\n\n<p><strong>Avoid</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dronedarone — increased risk of sudden death reported with concurrent digoxin; avoid coadministration when possible.</li>\n<li>Dofetilide — associated with higher torsades risk when given with digoxin; avoid or use only with cardiology oversight and close monitoring.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>Use with caution</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>PGP inhibitors and drugs that markedly raise digoxin levels (examples below). Measure baseline digoxin level, reduce dose (typical reductions noted), and monitor clinically and by serum concentration.</li>\n<li>Drugs that depress AV conduction (beta‑blockers, verapamil, diltiazem, ivabradine) — risk of bradycardia/heart block; monitor pulse and ECG.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr><th>Drug or class (examples)</th><th>Effect on digoxin</th><th>Practical response</th></tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Amiodarone, quinidine, propafenone, verapamil, erythromycin, itraconazole</td><td>Often ↑ >50%</td><td>Measure level before/after; reduce digoxin ≈30–50% or adjust frequency; monitor closely.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Moderate inhibitors (diltiazem, indomethacin, trimethoprim)</td><td>↑ <50%</td><td>Consider dose reduction ≈15–30% and monitor.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Inducers (rifampin, St. John’s Wort, some antisecretory agents)</td><td>↓ concentrations</td><td>Measure levels; may require 20–40% dose increase if clinically indicated.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drugs that reduce renal function (NSAIDs, ACEi/ARBs, diuretics)</td><td>Can impair digoxin excretion</td><td>Monitor renal function and digoxin level; adjust dose as needed.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout clinical\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical note</span><p>Many interacting effects are variable. Obtain a baseline digoxin concentration, reassess after initiating or stopping interacting drugs, and individualize dose changes to levels and clinical response.</p></div>\n\n<div class=\"medlens-callout patient\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Patient counseling</span><p>Tell patients to report nausea, visual changes, dizziness, palpitations, or syncope. Ask about herbal/traditional remedies (eg, St. John’s Wort, ginseng, Chan Su), which can change levels or interfere with assays.</p></div>",
