    "contraindications": {
      "summaryHtml": "<ul><li>Hypersensitivity to formoterol</li><li>Hypersensitivity to formulation components</li><li>Contraindications depend on the specific product.</li><li>Generally include:</li><li>Use of a LABA such as formoterol without concomitant ICS therapy is contraindicated/inappropriate for asthma management.</li></ul>",
      "detailsHtml": "<p>Contraindications depend on the specific product.</p><p>Generally include:</p><ul><li>Hypersensitivity to formoterol</li><li>Hypersensitivity to formulation components</li></ul><h4>Asthma</h4><p><strong>Use of a LABA such as formoterol without concomitant ICS therapy is contraindicated/inappropriate for asthma management.</strong></p><p>Fixed ICS-formoterol therapy should be used when formoterol is required for asthma.</p><h4>Dry-Powder Products</h4><p>Some dry-powder formulations contain lactose that may contain milk proteins.</p><p>Severe milk-protein hypersensitivity may therefore be a contraindication for specific products.</p><p>Always check the specific inhaler formulation.</p><p>---</p>"
    },
    "warnings": {
      "summaryHtml": "<ul><li>Deteriorating asthma</li><li>Poor adherence</li><li>Poor inhaler technique</li><li>Inadequate anti-inflammatory treatment</li></ul>",
      "detailsHtml": "<h4>LABA Monotherapy in Asthma</h4><p>This is the most important warning.</p><p><strong>Do not use formoterol without ICS therapy in patients with asthma.</strong></p><p>LABA monotherapy does not adequately treat the underlying airway inflammation and is associated with increased risk of serious asthma-related outcomes.</p><p>GINA recommends that people with asthma receive <strong>ICS-containing treatment</strong>. (<a href=\"https://ginasthma.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/GINA-2026-Strategy-Report-WMS.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Global Initiative for Asthma - GINA</a>)</p><p>---</p><h4>Do Not Confuse Formoterol Alone With ICS-Formoterol</h4><p>Formoterol&#39;s rapid onset does <strong>not</strong> mean that single-agent formoterol should be used as the preferred asthma reliever.</p><p>GINA&#39;s AIR strategy specifically involves:</p><p><strong>ICS + formoterol</strong></p><p>The corticosteroid component simultaneously treats the increased airway inflammation associated with worsening symptoms. (<a href=\"https://ginasthma.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/GINA-2026-Strategy-Report-WMS.pdf?utm_source=chatgpt.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Global Initiative for Asthma - GINA</a>)</p><p>---</p><h4>Excessive Use</h4><p>Do not exceed the recommended product-specific dose.</p><p>Increasing requirements for bronchodilator therapy may indicate:</p><ul><li>Deteriorating asthma</li><li>Poor adherence</li><li>Poor inhaler technique</li><li>Inadequate anti-inflammatory treatment</li><li>Acute exacerbation</li></ul><p>The patient should be reassessed rather than simply increasing LABA exposure.</p><p>---</p><h4>Cardiovascular Disease</h4><p>Use cautiously in patients with:</p><ul><li>Ischemic heart disease</li><li>Significant arrhythmias</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Cardiomyopathy</li><li>Other significant cardiovascular disease</li></ul><p>&beta;<sub>2</sub> stimulation may cause clinically important cardiovascular effects in susceptible patients.</p><p>---</p><h4>Hypokalemia</h4><p>&beta;<sub>2</sub> agonists can shift potassium intracellularly and produce transient:</p><p><strong>Hypokalemia</strong></p><p>Risk may increase with:</p><ul><li>High &beta;<sub>2</sub>-agonist doses</li><li>Loop diuretics</li><li>Thiazide diuretics</li><li>Systemic corticosteroids</li><li>Hypoxemia</li></ul><p>Severe hypokalemia may increase arrhythmia risk.</p><p>---</p><h4>Hyperglycemia</h4><p>&beta;<sub>2</sub> agonists may transiently increase blood glucose.</p><p>Use appropriate monitoring in patients with poorly controlled diabetes or other relevant risk factors.</p><p>---</p><h4>Hyperthyroidism</h4><p>Use cautiously because sympathomimetic effects may be more pronounced.</p><p>---</p><h4>Paradoxical Bronchospasm</h4><p>Rarely, inhaled formoterol may cause immediate worsening of bronchospasm.</p><p>If paradoxical bronchospasm occurs:</p><ul><li>Stop the offending inhaled product</li><li>Treat bronchospasm immediately with appropriate alternative therapy</li><li>Reassess the treatment regimen</li></ul><p>---</p><h4>Drug Interactions</h4><p>Use caution with:</p><p><strong>Other sympathomimetics</strong></p><p>May increase cardiovascular and adrenergic adverse effects.</p><p><strong>Nonselective beta-blockers</strong></p><p>May antagonize bronchodilation and provoke bronchospasm.</p><p>Examples:</p><ul><li>Propranolol</li><li>Nadolol</li></ul><p>Cardioselective beta-blockers may sometimes be necessary for compelling cardiovascular indications, but require individualized assessment.</p><p><strong>MAO inhibitors and tricyclic antidepressants</strong></p><p>May potentiate cardiovascular effects of sympathomimetics.</p><p><strong>Non-potassium-sparing diuretics</strong></p><p>May worsen &beta;<sub>2</sub>-agonist-associated hypokalemia.</p><p><strong>QT-prolonging medications</strong></p><p>May increase arrhythmia risk in susceptible patients.</p><p>---</p>"
    },
    "sideEffects": {
      "summaryHtml": "<ul><li>Tremor</li><li>Headache</li><li>Nervousness</li><li>Dizziness</li></ul>",
      "detailsHtml": "<p>Common adverse reactions reported with inhaled formoterol include the following:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Tremor</li>\n  <li>Headache</li>\n  <li>Nervousness</li>\n  <li>Dizziness</li>\n  <li>Muscle cramps</li>\n  <li>Throat irritation and cough</li>\n  <li>Palpitations or tachycardia</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"medlens-callout clinical\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical note</span><p>Metabolic changes such as <strong>hypokalemia</strong> and <strong>hyperglycemia</strong> have been reported. These are more likely with excessive doses, frequent administration, or when combined with other sympathomimetic agents.</p></div>\n<div class=\"medlens-callout warning\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical caution</span><p>Serious but uncommon reactions that require immediate attention include:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Paradoxical bronchospasm (worsening wheeze or shortness of breath immediately after inhalation)</li>\n  <li>Severe hypersensitivity reactions</li>\n  <li>Significant cardiac arrhythmias or marked increases in heart rate/ blood pressure</li>\n  <li>Severe hypokalemia</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If any severe reaction occurs after inhalation, stop the medication and seek urgent medical care.</p></div>\n<div class=\"medlens-callout clinical\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Clinical note</span><p>Clinically important cardiovascular effects are uncommon when the drug is used at recommended inhaled doses, but caution is appropriate for patients with cardiovascular disease, arrhythmias, or conditions that predispose to hypokalemia or hyperglycemia.</p></div>\n<div class=\"medlens-callout patient\"><span class=\"medlens-callout-label\">Patient counseling</span><p>Advise patients to report new or worsening wheeze, chest pain, palpitations, severe tremor, lightheadedness, muscle weakness or symptoms suggesting high blood glucose. Counsel patients not to exceed the prescribed dose and to tell clinicians about other sympathomimetic drugs they are taking.</p></div>"
    },
